WEBVTT

00:03.840 --> 00:04.160
All right.

00:04.160 --> 00:06.080
So now let's say that we have the following data.

00:06.080 --> 00:08.000
We have products and their sales.

00:08.000 --> 00:10.520
If you want now to go and rank your products.

00:10.720 --> 00:16.520
First you have to sort the data based on something like for example ranking the products based on their

00:16.520 --> 00:17.040
sales.

00:17.040 --> 00:22.360
So that means SQL is going to go and start sorting your data from the highest to the lowest.

00:22.360 --> 00:28.480
So sorting the data is always the first thing SQL has to do before ranking anything.

00:28.480 --> 00:30.920
Now, in order to rank our data, we have two methods.

00:31.040 --> 00:34.480
The first method, we call it the integer based ranking.

00:34.480 --> 00:41.240
So that means SQL can go and assign for each row an integer a whole number based on the position of

00:41.240 --> 00:41.800
the row.

00:41.800 --> 00:47.240
So now by looking to the example the first row, we have the product E with the sales 70 is going to

00:47.240 --> 00:48.760
be ranked number one.

00:48.800 --> 00:50.800
Then the next row the product B.

00:50.840 --> 00:54.080
With the 30 sales we will get the rank number two.

00:54.240 --> 00:57.680
Then the next one can be three four and the last one going to be five.

00:57.720 --> 01:04.280
So that means SQL here is assigning an integer for each row based on their position in the sorted list.

01:04.280 --> 01:07.400
So this method we call it integer based ranking.

01:07.400 --> 01:08.920
Now let's go to the second method.

01:08.920 --> 01:11.360
We have the percentage based ranking.

01:11.360 --> 01:17.880
So in this method SQL going to go first and calculate the relative position of the row compared to all

01:17.880 --> 01:18.520
others.

01:18.520 --> 01:21.240
And then assign a percentage for each row.

01:21.240 --> 01:25.480
So here in the output is going to start assigning percentages instead of integer.

01:25.720 --> 01:28.640
And we're going to have a scale from 0 to 1.

01:28.640 --> 01:33.000
So now if you go and compare both of the methods you can see that on the left side on the integer based

01:33.000 --> 01:37.000
ranking we have discrete distinct values.

01:37.000 --> 01:41.280
So it starts from one, then two three and end up in this example by five.

01:41.280 --> 01:45.280
So it's really depends on how many rows do we have in the results.

01:45.280 --> 01:46.200
So it could be five.

01:46.240 --> 01:48.760
It could be 505 million and so on.

01:48.760 --> 01:53.480
But in the right side we have always the same scale from 1 to 0.

01:53.480 --> 01:56.880
So between 0 and 1 we have infinite number of data points.

01:57.120 --> 02:03.800
And this scale we call it a normalized scale or we call it continuous scale, continuous values.

02:03.800 --> 02:06.160
So now the question is when to use which method.

02:06.240 --> 02:10.640
So for example for the percentage based ranking it is great to answer such questions.

02:10.880 --> 02:14.480
Find the top 20% products based on their sales.

02:14.480 --> 02:20.480
So this method is a great way in order to understand the contributions of data values to the overall

02:20.520 --> 02:21.120
total.

02:21.120 --> 02:26.240
And we call this kind of analysis a distribution analysis, where in the other hand, in the integer

02:26.240 --> 02:30.880
based ranking, we can answer questions like find the top three products.

02:30.880 --> 02:35.800
So with this question we are not interesting about the contributions of each product to the overall

02:35.840 --> 02:36.440
total.

02:36.440 --> 02:40.680
We are just interested in the position of the value within a list.

02:40.680 --> 02:44.000
So this is as well very commonly used analysis and reporting.

02:44.000 --> 02:46.800
We call it top button and analysis.

02:46.800 --> 02:50.320
So now let's group up our ranking functions based on those two methods.

02:50.520 --> 02:56.640
For the first group in the integer based ranking we have four functions row number, rank, dense Denserank

02:56.680 --> 02:57.440
and entail.

02:57.480 --> 03:01.840
But on the other hand, we have only two functions that generate percentage based ranking.

03:01.960 --> 03:05.160
We have the list and as well the percentile.

03:05.480 --> 03:11.400
So now that was an introduction an overview of those methods and how we group up those ranking functions.

03:11.440 --> 03:15.120
Next we're going to go and learn about the syntax of the ranking functions.

03:15.120 --> 03:17.040
Most of them follow the same rules.

03:17.040 --> 03:19.600
So for example we start always with the function name.

03:19.600 --> 03:21.200
So we have here the rank.

03:21.400 --> 03:24.080
But as you can see we don't use any expressions.

03:24.080 --> 03:27.480
So they don't allow you to use any argument inside it.

03:27.520 --> 03:28.840
It must be empty.

03:28.840 --> 03:31.320
So this is the first rule using rank functions.

03:31.320 --> 03:33.320
Then about the definition of the window.

03:33.560 --> 03:36.680
As usual, the partition by it is an optional thing.

03:36.680 --> 03:38.200
You can use it or leave it.

03:38.200 --> 03:40.600
And now to the second part we have the order by.

03:40.640 --> 03:42.360
It is as well required.

03:42.360 --> 03:48.040
So you must order the data or sort your data in order to do ranking, so you cannot leave it empty.

03:48.280 --> 03:53.880
So that means for the definition of the window at least we should have an order by for example here

03:53.920 --> 03:54.560
sales.

03:54.560 --> 03:56.400
So we cannot leave it empty.

03:56.440 --> 03:56.800
All right.

03:56.800 --> 04:00.960
So the two requirements you cannot use any expressions for those functions.

04:00.960 --> 04:04.000
And as well you have to sort your data using order by.

04:04.040 --> 04:04.240
Okay.

04:04.280 --> 04:06.360
So now let's have an overview of all functions.

04:06.360 --> 04:09.480
So as you can see all those functions are ranking functions.

04:09.480 --> 04:14.200
And almost all of them don't allow to use any expressions inside them.

04:14.400 --> 04:16.960
Besides this function here we have the internal.

04:17.160 --> 04:19.120
It accepts a number inside it.

04:19.120 --> 04:21.000
So that means you cannot use it empty.

04:21.040 --> 04:22.800
You should use a number inside it.

04:22.800 --> 04:24.960
All others must be empty.

04:24.960 --> 04:30.280
So now for the partition by all of them are optional, and for the order by all of them are required.

04:30.280 --> 04:34.000
So you must use order by and the frame clause.

04:34.280 --> 04:40.360
They are not allowed to use in the ranking functions, so you cannot change the definition of the frame

04:40.480 --> 04:41.720
inside the window function.

04:41.720 --> 04:45.880
So now what we're going to do as usual we're going to go and deep dive into all of those functions in

04:45.880 --> 04:51.440
order to understand when to use them and what are their use cases and as well practice in SQL.

04:51.440 --> 04:54.280
So we're going to start with the first one, the row number.
